"""
OHT Fleet Controller  —  loops that follow the REAL rail of the bay
=====================================================================
Run in Omniverse / Isaac Sim:  Window > Script Editor > paste > Run (Ctrl+Enter),
they start moving right away.  Re-running the script rebuilds the fleet cleanly.
To stop it:  run   import builtins; builtins._oht_fleet.stop()

What the rail in oht_animation.usd actually looks like (measured from the
straight rail pieces; the curve meshes sit in the Orbital Line payload):

        Y_TOP  -18.441  <======================================  top through-line
                 |  ^ curve                               curve ^ |
                 |  (joins 0.8 m NORTH of the lane)               |
        lane   -20.241  ------------------------------------------
                 |                                                |
        lane   -22.041  ------------------------------------------
                 |                                                |
        lane   -24.141  ------------------------------------------
                 |                                                |
        Y_BOT  -25.891  ==========================================  bottom through-line
              X_WEST 7.698                                 X_EAST 29.398

  * The curve between a bay lane and a vertical rail always bends NORTH:
    it meets the vertical 0.8 m north of the lane, at both ends.
    So an OHT can only
        - LEAVE a lane by curving north onto the vertical, and
        - ENTER a lane from a vertical it is driving SOUTH on.
    Going east (counter-clockwise loop) that means: lane eastbound ->
    LEFT turn north at the east end ... LEFT turn west onto the top line ...
    LEFT turn south at the west end ... LEFT turn east back into the lane.
  * The old Loop_A (lane -24.141 east, lane -22.041 west) was impossible on
    this rail: at the east end it turned LEFT into lane -22.041 and at the
    west end LEFT out of it, but both curves there are RIGHT-turn curves for
    that direction (they bend the other way). That is the wrong turning you saw.
  * Every lane therefore returns over the top line, so the loops SHARE rail
    (top line + the upper part of both verticals). The traffic logic now sees
    OHTs of other loops on shared rail and lets them zip at the merges.

Changes compared to the previous version
  * ROUTES are built with bay_loop(lane_y): lane -> east vertical -> top line
    -> west vertical -> lane. One loop per bay lane.
  * CHECK_TURNS: every corner is checked against the real curve direction;
    a loop that would turn the wrong way is reported and not used.
  * SharedTraffic: distance keeping across loops on shared rail, merge
    priority (who is closer to the merge goes first), collision-free start.
  * Head-on use of the same rail by two loops is reported.

Everything else (curves, bogies, braking, hoist, claws, cables, magazines)
works exactly as before.
"""

